title mycelium Lapse r4 — a real lapsed reservation reads true (app == awk); the arc closes
prompt loop card: keep going Lindy-first crux-first; each finished increment its own signed round; send often
think r4 closes the Lapse arc by carrying the reader onto a real on-disk Cord record a keeper can open and walk, mirroring pledge_true/braid_true. The property proven: the LAPSED recipient — the one the coins never reached because the order's clock ran out — is true to the bytes. The corpus tools/fixtures/lapse_dag.bron is produced reproducibly by mycelium/lapse_fixture_gen.rye (genesis Alice 200; Alice pledges 100 to Bob under deadline 2 at round 1; Alice posts it at round 2, yet the post rides order position 2 so the pledge LAPSES, hundred home to Alice, post a no-op; Alice pledges 100 to Carol under deadline 9 at round 3, left open), @embedFile-bound through a mycelium/corpora/ symlink.
think The crux is two tools, one answer. mycelium/lapse_true parses through cord_bron, rebuilds the Dag under cord.add (re-verifying every signature), resolves through lapse, and reports the triple (lapsed recipient · lapsed count · open count): 55154f42… (Bob) 1 1. The independent tools/fixtures/lapse_dag_truth.awk reads the same bytes and — crucially — ticks the SAME clock: it tracks the running file position and, before applying each block's fact, lapses every open pledge whose deadline (read little-endian from the pledge body's chars 35..50) the position has reached (deadline <= pos), exactly as lapse.fold_lapse ticks before it applies. So the app's full commit tie-break and the awk's file-order accounting name the identical lapsed recipient; app.out == awk.out enforced in the witness.
think The awk had to parse the deadline field the pledge arc did not carry — a little-endian u64 over 8 hex bytes, built with a nibble table in BEGIN and a le64 helper. The fixture's deadline for pledge 1 renders as 0200000000000000 (LE = 2), reached at position 2 where the post sits, so the lapse is genuine, not asserted. The fixture is reproducible (the generator re-emits byte-identical bytes, diffed in the witness) and a tampered header refuses BadFormat.
think Conservation holds over the real bytes (balance + reserved sums to the 200 genesis issued); the lapsed pledge's intended recipient (Bob) holds 0 — the coins came home to Alice when the clock ran out — and the open pledge still holds 100 reserved. GREEN on the first run of each piece.
obs The Mycelium Lapse arc stands complete end to end — r1 the deadline crux (a late honor loses to expiry, decided once by the order's clock) · r2 travels as format lapse-v1 · r3 across a Knot (the epoch base keeps the absolute clock unbroken) · r4 reads true. This is Season D's sixth ledger arc (Cord · Knot · Tenure · Till · Purse · Pledge · Braid · Lapse), and TigerBeetle's third signature primitive (pending-transfer timeout) now reads the agreed order as its own law. TAME bans clean, width-check clean.
loom witness=mycelium_lapse_true_witness build=ok selftest=GREEN app_eq_awk=yes triple="55154f42… 1 1" reproducible=yes
